---
/*
  Built from the full deep-research → content-brief → content-create →
  humanizer pipeline output (07-landing-pages/content/utility-excavation-content.md).
  Promoted from the /services/utility-excavation-test draft after review.

  OPEN ITEM carried from the content file: the electrical burial-depth figure
  (6-24 in., NEC Table 300.5) is the confirmed NATIONAL baseline only — Oregon's
  own OESC amendment table couldn't be directly verified (403 error during
  research). Water/sewer/gas figures are solidly sourced from Lane County's own
  government page and don't carry the same hedge. Also unconfirmed: which party
  (D&D vs. property owner) initiates a new utility-tap application, and whether
  D&D has actually performed joint/gas/fiber trenching vs. offering it as a
  category.
*/
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import Hero from '../../components/Hero.astro';
import AboutSection from '../../components/AboutSection.astro';
import PhotoContentBlock from '../../components/PhotoContentBlock.astro';
import WhyChooseUs from '../../components/WhyChooseUs.astro';
import ServiceCard from '../../components/ServiceCard.astro';
import FAQ from '../../components/FAQ.astro';
import CTABlock from '../../components/CTABlock.astro';
import Gallery from '../../components/Gallery.astro';
import Testimonials from '../../components/Testimonials.astro';

const utilities = [
  { name: 'Water service lines', detail: 'A new water line run from the meter or main to a structure.' },
  { name: 'Sewer laterals', detail: 'The pipe connecting a structure to a public sewer main or, on a property without one, to a septic system.' },
  { name: 'Electrical conduit', detail: 'A new service, sub-panel run, or power line to a detached shop, barn, or outbuilding.' },
  { name: 'Gas lines', detail: 'New gas service to a structure, or an individual line to an outdoor appliance like a grill or a light.' },
  { name: 'Communications/fiber conduit', detail: 'Trenching for phone, cable, or fiber lines. Depth follows the individual ISP or utility’s own construction standard, no Oregon code figure exists for this one.' },
];

const depthTable = [
  { utility: 'Water service line', depth: '24 in. total (12 in. below Lane County’s 12 in. frost depth)', code: 'OPSC 609.1' },
  { utility: 'Sewer lateral', depth: '12 in. minimum ground cover, sloped ~1/4 in./ft', code: 'OPSC 718.1' },
  { utility: 'Gas line', depth: '18 in. standard (8 in. for outdoor-appliance lines, if approved)', code: 'ORSC G2415.12 / .12.1' },
  { utility: 'Electrical conduit', depth: '6–24 in., depending on wiring method — NEC national baseline; Oregon’s OESC amendment not directly confirmed', code: 'NEC Table 300.5' },
];

const faqs = [
  { question: 'How deep does a water line need to be buried in Oregon?', answer: '12 inches below Lane County’s own 12-inch frost depth, for a total minimum trench depth of 24 inches below grade, per Oregon Plumbing Specialty Code 609.1. Frost depth varies by county, so this exact total is specific to Lane County, not a statewide constant.' },
  { question: 'How deep does a sewer lateral need to be buried?', answer: '12 inches minimum ground cover, per OPSC 718.1, sloped at roughly 1/4 inch per foot toward the discharge point.' },
  { question: 'How deep does electrical conduit need to be buried?', answer: '6 to 24 inches, depending on the wiring method: rigid metal conduit shallowest, direct-buried cable deepest, per NEC Table 300.5, the national baseline Oregon adopts. Any Oregon-specific amendment to this baseline should be confirmed for an exact figure; treat the range above as the confirmed national standard, not a settled Oregon-specific number.' },
  { question: 'How deep does a gas line need to be buried?', answer: '18 inches minimum cover standard, per ORSC G2415.12. An individual outdoor-appliance line, a grill or a light, may run as shallow as 8 inches if approved by the local authority, per G2415.12.1.' },
  { question: 'Can water, sewer, electrical, and gas all go in the same trench?', answer: 'Yes, generally. Lane County permits multiple utilities sharing one trench without prohibition. The one code-stated rule: the water pipe’s bottom must sit at least 12 inches above the sewer pipe’s top where the two cross (OPSC 720.1). Individual utility companies may add their own separation specs on top of this, so confirm for a specific job.' },
  { question: 'Who calls Oregon811 before utility trenching starts?', answer: 'D&D Land Works does, before every dig. Oregon811 requires at least two business days’ advance notice, free of charge, enforced by the Oregon PUC. This is a separate call from the utility-company tap application.' },
  { question: 'Who calls the utility company for a new tap — me or the contractor?', answer: 'Typically, the property owner or their developer/builder initiates the tap request directly with the utility (EWEB, Springfield Utility Board, or a rural water district); the actual trenching from the main or meter to the structure is done separately by the excavation contractor. This varies by utility, and which party D&D itself coordinates with on a given job is confirmed per job, not a fixed practice.' },
  { question: 'What’s the difference between utility excavation and trenching and backfill?', answer: 'This page covers which utility is being run, why, and at what code-required depth: water, sewer, electrical, gas, and communications. Trenching & Backfill covers the general mechanics of opening and properly closing any trench, bedding, compaction in lifts, surface restoration, whether it’s for a utility line, drainage pipe, or a footing.' },
  { question: 'Does trenching under a driveway or patio cost more than trenching through open ground?', answer: 'Yes. Trenching through existing hardscape requires saw-cutting, disposal of the removed material, and surface restoration that open-ground trenching doesn’t, a real cost driver stated honestly here rather than surfacing as a surprise mid-job. No Lane-County-exact multiplier exists; a free on-site estimate is the way to price a specific trench route.' },
];

const related = [
  { title: 'Excavation & Earthmoving', description: 'General cut-and-fill, trenching, and earthmoving mechanics, plus the fuller OSHA/Oregon811 picture.', href: '/services/excavation' },
  { title: 'Drainage Excavation', description: 'Buried trenching for water management, not utility service delivery.', href: '/services/drainage-excavation' },
  { title: 'Trenching & Backfill', description: 'The general mechanics of closing any trench properly.', href: '/services/trenching-backfill' },
  { title: 'Septic Install & Repair', description: 'The septic tank and drainfield itself.', href: '/services/septic-install-repairs' },
];

const demoReviews = [
  { name: 'Jordan M.', initial: 'JM', date: 'Aug 2026', stars: 5, text: 'David and his crew graded our lot fast and cleaned up everything before they left. Would hire again for any excavation work.' },
  { name: 'Casey R.', initial: 'CR', date: 'Jul 2026', stars: 5, text: 'Called about a drainage issue and had someone out within the week. Fixed the standing water problem for good.' },
  { name: 'Taylor B.', initial: 'TB', date: 'Jul 2026', stars: 4, text: 'Good work on our driveway repair, just took a little longer than expected to schedule.' },
  { name: 'Morgan K.', initial: 'MK', date: 'Jun 2026', stars: 5, text: 'Septic install went smoothly start to finish. Appreciated that David walked us through the whole process.' },
  { name: 'Riley S.', initial: 'RS', date: 'Jun 2026', stars: 5, text: 'Cleared almost an acre of brush for us ahead of a build. On time, fair price, no surprises.' },
  { name: 'Alex D.', initial: 'AD', date: 'May 2026', stars: 5, text: 'Foundation excavation was done right and on schedule. Highly recommend for any site prep work.' },
];

const anchorAreas = [
  { label: 'Eugene', href: '/locations/eugene' },
  { label: 'Springfield', href: '/locations/springfield' },
];
const otherAreas = [
  { label: 'Cottage Grove', href: '/locations/cottage-grove' },
  { label: 'Junction City', href: '/locations/junction-city' },
  { label: 'Creswell', href: '/locations/creswell' },
  { label: 'Veneta', href: '/locations/veneta' },
  { label: 'Florence', href: '/locations/florence' },
  { label: 'Oakridge', href: '/locations/oakridge' },
  { label: 'Coburg', href: '/locations/coburg' },
  { label: 'Lowell', href: '/locations/lowell' },
];

const serviceSchema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@graph': [
    {
      '@type': 'Service',
      '@id': 'https://www.ddlandworks.com/services/utility-excavation/#service',
      name: 'Utility Excavation',
      serviceType: 'Utility Excavation',
      description: "Utility excavation is trenching to bring a new water service line, sewer lateral, electrical conduit, or gas line to a structure. Required burial depth in Lane County, Oregon depends on the utility. D&D Land Works calls Oregon 811 before every dig and provides utility excavation for residential and commercial properties throughout Eugene, Springfield, and Lane County, Oregon.",
      url: 'https://www.ddlandworks.com/services/utility-excavation/',
      provider: { '@id': 'https://www.ddlandworks.com/#business' },
      areaServed: ['Lane County, Oregon', 'Eugene, Oregon', 'Springfield, Oregon', 'Cottage Grove, Oregon', 'Junction City, Oregon', 'Creswell, Oregon', 'Veneta, Oregon', 'Florence, Oregon', 'Oakridge, Oregon', 'Coburg, Oregon', 'Lowell, Oregon'],
    },
    {
      '@type': 'BreadcrumbList',
      itemListElement: [
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 1, name: 'Home', item: 'https://www.ddlandworks.com/' },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 2, name: 'Services', item: 'https://www.ddlandworks.com/services/' },
        { '@type': 'ListItem', position: 3, name: 'Utility Excavation', item: 'https://www.ddlandworks.com/services/utility-excavation/' },
      ],
    },
  ],
};
---

<BaseLayout
	title="Utility Excavation in Eugene & Lane County, OR | D&D Land Works"
	description="Water, sewer, electrical & gas line trenching in Eugene, Springfield & Lane County, OR. Licensed, bonded, free estimates: 541-401-8726."
>
	<Hero
		eyebrow="Service"
		headline="Utility Excavation in Eugene, Springfield & Lane County, Oregon"
		subheadline="Water, sewer, power, and gas lines all have to go in at the right depth, with the right bedding, and clear of everything already buried nearby. D&D Land Works calls locates first and digs second, every time."
		primaryCta={{ label: 'Call 541-401-8726', href: 'tel:5414018726' }}
		secondaryCta={{ label: 'Free Estimate', href: '/contact' }}
	/>
	<!-- About + AI citation block combined -->
	<AboutSection
		ctaLabel="Get a Free Estimate"
		ctaHref="/contact"
		eyebrow="Utility Excavation in Lane County, OR"
		headlineHtml="Professional <span class='text-brand-blue'>Utility Excavation</span> in Eugene, Springfield &amp; Lane County"
		descriptionHtml="<p>Utility excavation is trenching to bring a new water service line, sewer lateral, electrical conduit, or gas line to a structure &mdash; a different job than general excavation (creating space for a foundation) or drainage excavation (managing water). Required burial depth in Lane County, Oregon depends on the utility: water lines need 24 inches total (OPSC 609.1), sewer laterals need 12 inches of cover (OPSC 718.1), gas lines need 18 inches (ORSC G2415.12), and electrical conduit needs 6&ndash;24 inches depending on wiring method (NEC Table 300.5).</p><p>D&amp;D Land Works calls Oregon 811 before every dig and provides utility excavation for residential and commercial properties throughout Eugene, Springfield, and Lane County under Oregon <strong class='font-semibold text-ink'>CCB #261742</strong>. Free estimates are available.</p>"
	/>
	<!-- What it covers -->
	<section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-16">
		<h2 class="font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-3xl text-ink text-center">
			What Does Utility Excavation Cover?
		</h2>
		<p class="mt-4 text-center text-slate-700 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
			Every job here starts the same way: a call to Oregon811 before anything gets dug, so existing lines get marked first. From there, the trenching is organized by which utility is going in and why.
		</p>
		<div class="mt-10 grid gap-4 sm:grid-cols-2">
			{utilities.map((u) => (
				<div class="card">
					<p class="font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-sm text-brand-blue">{u.name}</p>
					<p class="mt-2 text-sm text-slate-600">{u.detail}</p>
				</div>
			))}
		</div>
	</section>
	<!-- Depth table -->
	<section class="bg-white border-y border-slate-200">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-4xl px-4 py-16">
			<h2 class="font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-3xl text-ink text-center">
				How Deep Does a Utility Line Need to Be Buried in Oregon?
			</h2>
			<p class="mt-4 text-center text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
				There isn&rsquo;t one answer. Every utility has its own code-required minimum depth, genuinely different from each other.
			</p>
			<div class="mt-8 overflow-x-auto">
				<table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
					<thead>
						<tr class="border-b border-slate-300 text-left">
							<th class="py-2 pr-4 font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-ink">Utility</th>
							<th class="py-2 pr-4 font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-ink">Minimum Depth</th>
							<th class="py-2 font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-ink">Code</th>
						</tr>
					</thead>
					<tbody>
						{depthTable.map((row) => (
							<tr class="border-b border-slate-200">
								<td class="py-3 pr-4 font-semibold text-ink whitespace-nowrap">{row.utility}</td>
								<td class="py-3 pr-4 text-slate-600">{row.depth}</td>
								<td class="py-3 text-brand-blue font-semibold whitespace-nowrap">{row.code}</td>
							</tr>
						))}
					</tbody>
				</table>
			</div>
			<p class="mt-6 text-sm text-slate-600 text-center max-w-2xl mx-auto">
				The water figure is Lane-County-specific: frost depth varies by county. The electrical figure carries the one hedge on this page: it&rsquo;s the correct national baseline Oregon builds from, but not a substitute for confirming the current Oregon Electrical Specialty Code amendment against a specific job.
			</p>
			<div class="mt-6 text-center">
				<a href="/contact" class="btn btn-cta">Get a Free On-Site Estimate</a>
			</div>
		</div>
	</section>
	<!-- Joint trenching -->
	<PhotoContentBlock
		eyebrow="Joint Trenching"
		headline="Can Multiple Utilities Go in the Same Trench?"
		image="/hero-bg-1.webp"
		imageAlt="Open utility trench ready for water, sewer, and electrical lines in Lane County"
		imageSide="left"
		badgeValue="12 in"
		badgeLabel="Water Must Clear Sewer by This Much (OPSC 720.1)"
		ctaLabel="Ask About a Joint Trench"
		ctaHref="/contact"
		bodyHtml="<p>Yes, generally. Lane County code allows multiple utilities to share one trench without prohibition, so water, sewer, electrical, and gas can go in together instead of four separate digs. The one code-stated rule: where a water line and a sewer line cross, the water pipe&rsquo;s bottom must sit at least 12 inches above the sewer pipe&rsquo;s top (OPSC 720.1).</p><p>Individual utility companies, EWEB, Springfield Utility Board, and NW Natural among them, may add their own separation specs on top of that. Buried non-metallic lines also need tracer wire so they can be located later: blue for water, green for sanitary sewer, yellow for gas.</p>"
	/>
	<!-- Who calls who + hitting an existing line — each answer gets its own photo block, alternating sides -->
	<PhotoContentBlock
		eyebrow="Who Calls Who"
		headline="Who Calls Before Digging, and Who Calls the Utility Company?"
		image="/hero-bg-2.webp"
		imageAlt="Utility locate flags marking underground lines before trenching"
		imageSide="right"
		badgeValue="2 Calls"
		badgeLabel="Oregon 811 + Utility Tap, Different Parties"
		ctaLabel="Get a Free Estimate"
		ctaHref="/contact"
		bodyHtml="<p>Two different calls, to two different parties, for two different reasons. D&amp;D Land Works calls Oregon811 before every dig, the free, mandatory locate request requiring at least two business days&rsquo; notice so existing lines get marked first.</p><p>Requesting a new utility tap is separate: typically, the property owner or their developer/builder initiates that application directly with the utility, and the trenching from the main or meter to the structure is done separately by the excavation contractor. Which party handles the tap-application call on a given job varies and is confirmed per job.</p>"
	/>
	<PhotoContentBlock
		eyebrow="Hitting an Existing Line"
		headline="What Happens If You Hit an Existing Line?"
		image="/hero-bg-3.webp"
		imageAlt="Excavator working carefully near marked utility lines in Lane County"
		imageSide="left"
		badgeValue="Work Stops"
		badgeLabel="If an Unmarked Line Is Hit"
		ctaLabel="Get a Free Estimate"
		ctaHref="/contact"
		bodyHtml="<p>Work stops in that spot. If a trencher or excavator exposes an unmarked line mid-dig, digging there stops until the situation is assessed and, if needed, the line&rsquo;s owner is contacted.</p><p>This is exactly why D&amp;D calls Oregon811 at least two business days before starting any utility trench: a line marked in advance is far safer than one found by hitting it.</p>"
	/>
	<!-- Cost -->
	<PhotoContentBlock
		eyebrow="Cost"
		headline="How Much Does Utility Trenching Cost?"
		image="/hero-bg-4.webp"
		imageAlt="Utility trench cut across a driveway in Lane County, Oregon"
		imageSide="right"
		badgeValue="Saw-Cut Adds Cost"
		badgeLabel="Hardscape Trenching vs. Open Ground"
		ctaLabel="Request a Free Estimate"
		ctaHref="/contact"
		bodyHtml="<p>Cost depends on which utility is being run, how deep it has to go, and what sits above it. No verified per-foot figure exists yet for this specific niche.</p><p>One driver applies across all four types: trenching through an existing driveway, patio, or sidewalk commonly costs more than trenching through open ground, since it adds saw-cutting, material disposal, and surface restoration open-ground trenching doesn&rsquo;t need.</p>"
	/>
	<!-- Utility excavation vs trenching & backfill -->
	<PhotoContentBlock
		eyebrow="Utility Excavation vs. Trenching & Backfill"
		headline="What's the Difference Between Utility Excavation and Trenching & Backfill?"
		image="/hero-bg-1.webp"
		imageAlt="Properly backfilled and compacted utility trench in Lane County"
		imageSide="left"
		badgeValue="2 Trades"
		badgeLabel="What's Run vs. How the Trench Is Closed"
		ctaLabel="See Trenching & Backfill"
		ctaHref="/services/trenching-backfill"
		bodyHtml="<p>This page covers which utility is being run, why, and at what code-required depth.</p><p><a href='/services/trenching-backfill' class='text-brand-blue underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-brand-blue-dark'>Trenching &amp; Backfill</a> covers the general mechanics of opening and properly closing any trench &mdash; bedding, compaction in lifts, and surface restoration &mdash; whether that trench is for a utility line, a drainage pipe, or a footing.</p>"
	/>
	<PhotoContentBlock
		eyebrow="Utility Excavation in Lane County, OR"
		headline="Why Do Lane County Homeowners and Builders Trust D&D Land Works for Utility Excavation?"
		image="/hero-bg-2.webp"
		imageAlt="D&D Land Works trenching for utility lines in Lane County, Oregon"
		imageSide="right"
		badgeValue="CCB #261742"
		badgeLabel="Licensed & Bonded"
		ctaLabel="Get a Free Estimate"
		ctaHref="/contact"
		bodyHtml="<p>D&amp;D Land Works is licensed and bonded under Oregon CCB #261742 &mdash; a credential you can verify yourself at the CCB&rsquo;s own license lookup before you ever pick up the phone. Every utility trench is dug to the code-required depth for that specific line, and Oregon 811 gets called at least two business days before equipment ever moves.</p><p>David Deggelman scopes every job in person and confirms which utilities are going in, at what depth, and whether they can share a trench, before anything&rsquo;s quoted. If you&rsquo;re bringing water, sewer, electrical, or gas service to a new structure anywhere in Eugene, Springfield, or the rest of Lane County, <a href='/contact' class='text-brand-blue underline underline-offset-2 hover:text-brand-blue-dark'>contact D&amp;D Land Works</a> to schedule your free, on-site estimate.</p>"
	/>
	<WhyChooseUs
		descriptionHtml="Every utility trench starts with a call to Oregon 811, gets cut to the code-required depth for that specific line, and is scoped by David in person before anything's quoted."
	/>
	<!-- Service areas -->
	<section class="bg-slate-50 border-b border-slate-200">
		<div class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 py-16">
			<h2 class="font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-3xl text-ink text-center">
				What Areas of Lane County Does D&amp;D Land Works Serve?
			</h2>
			<p class="mt-4 text-center text-slate-700 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
				D&amp;D Land Works is <strong class="text-brand-blue">based near Eugene, Oregon</strong>, and serves homeowners, builders, and developers across Lane County. From Eugene and Springfield to Cottage Grove, Junction City, Creswell, and Veneta, we handle licensed and bonded excavation, grading, and DEQ certified septic work.
			</p>
			<div class="mt-10 grid gap-8 lg:grid-cols-2 items-start">
				<div class="aspect-4/3 overflow-hidden rounded-(--radius-brand-card) border border-slate-200 shadow-sm">
					<iframe
						title="D&D Land Works service area map, Lane County, Oregon"
						src="https://maps.google.com/maps?q=Lane+County,+Oregon&z=9&output=embed"
						width="100%"
						height="100%"
						style="border:0;"
						loading="lazy"
						referrerpolicy="no-referrer-when-downgrade"
					></iframe>
				</div>
				<div class="flex flex-col gap-6">
					<div class="card">
						<p class="font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-wide text-sm text-ink mb-3">
							Our Service Area Includes
						</p>
						<p class="leading-relaxed">
							{anchorAreas.map((area, i) => (
								<>
									<a href={area.href} class="font-semibold text-brand-blue hover:text-brand-blue-dark underline underline-offset-2">{area.label}</a>
									{i < anchorAreas.length - 1 && <span class="text-slate-400"> &middot; </span>}
								</>
							))}
							<span class="text-slate-400"> &middot; </span>
							{otherAreas.map((area, i) => (
								<>
									<a href={area.href} class="text-slate-700 hover:text-brand-blue">{area.label}</a>
									{i < otherAreas.length - 1 && <span class="text-slate-400"> &middot; </span>}
								</>
							))}
						</p>
					</div>
					<p class="text-slate-700 leading-relaxed">
						David walks every site in person before quoting, so pricing accounts for the actual
						soil, slope, and access on your property, not a regional average. Licensed and bonded
						under CCB #261742, DEQ certified for septic install and repair, with equipment and
						crew already local to Lane County.
					</p>
					<a href="/service-area" class="btn btn-cta self-start">View All Service Areas &rarr;</a>
				</div>
			</div>
			<p class="mt-6 text-center text-sm text-slate-600">
				Don&rsquo;t see your town? Call <a href="tel:5414018726" class="font-semibold text-brand-blue">541-401-8726</a> &mdash; the list above isn&rsquo;t exhaustive of every address D&amp;D Land Works will drive to.
			</p>
		</div>
	</section>
	<Gallery />
	<Testimonials
		businessName="D&D Land Works"
		rating={4.83}
		reviewCount={demoReviews.length}
		reviews={demoReviews}
	/>
	<FAQ items={faqs} />
	<section class="mx-auto max-w-6xl px-4 pb-16">
		<h2 class="font-display font-bold uppercase tracking-tight text-2xl text-ink text-center">
			Related Services
		</h2>
		<div class="mt-8 grid gap-6 sm:grid-cols-2 lg:grid-cols-4">
			{related.map((service) => <ServiceCard {...service} />)}
		</div>
	</section>
	<CTABlock
		headline="Get Your Utility Line In"
		subheadline="Water, sewer, electrical, and gas lines each have their own code-required depth. Free estimate for utility excavation anywhere in Lane County."
	/>
</BaseLayout>
<script type="application/ld+json" set:html={JSON.stringify(serviceSchema)} />
